{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וישא אהרן את ידיו", :translit "vayisa aharon et yadav", :gloss "And Aaron lifted his hands"}], :mechanisms [], :frame {:text "The priestly gifts (matnot kehunah) are enumerated across several passages: Numbers 18:8–19 lists terumah, the breast and shoulder of shelamim, the firstborn of animals, and other portions; Deuteronomy 18:3 specifies the foreleg, cheeks, and maw of a non-sacred slaughter as the priest's due. These gifts are understood as a perpetual entitlement of the priesthood.", :sources ["Numbers 18:8–19" "Deuteronomy 18:3"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויברכם", :translit "vayivarchem", :gloss "And he blessed them"} {:he "רגלי חסידיו ישמור", :translit "raglei chasidav yishmor", :gloss "the feet of His faithful ones He guards"} {:he "בעלותך לראות", :translit "be'alotkha lirot", :gloss "when you go up to see"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Three-fold blessing aligned with three offering types via verbal links in prooftexts"}], :frame {:text "The three blessings are the Priestly Blessing of Numbers 6:24–26: 'May the LORD bless you and guard you,' 'May the LORD shine His face upon you,' 'May the LORD lift His face toward you and grant you peace.' Leviticus 9:22 is taken as the inaugural occasion for their recitation. The correspondence rests on shared roots: 'guard' (sh-m-r) links to sin-offering, the language of ascending (a-l-h) links to burnt-offering, and 'peace' (shalom) links to peace-offerings. The first prooftext is Psalms 147:6; the second is Isaiah 30:2; the connection of 'shine' (y'orer) to ascending is drawn in Sotah 38a.", :sources ["Numbers 6:24–26" "Leviticus 9:22" "Psalms 147:6" "Isaiah 30:2" "Sotah 38a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Three-fold blessing aligned with three offering types via verbal links in prooftexts", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:22:27.551746352Z", :model "glm-5.2"}